# Elixir Koans - 03 Numbers

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
```

## Intro

Why is the number six so scared?

Because seven eight nine!

We should get to know numbers a bit more!

## Is an integer equal to its float equivalent?

```elixir
assert 1 == 1.0 == ___
```

## Is an integer threequal to its float equivalent?

```elixir
assert 1 === 1.0 == ___
```

## Revisit division with threequal

```elixir
assert 2 / 2 === ___
```

## Another way to divide

```elixir
assert div(5, 2) == ___
```

## What remains or: The Case of the Missing Modulo Operator (%)

```elixir
assert rem(5, 2) == ___
```

## Other math operators may produce this

```elixir
assert 2 * 2 === ___
```

## Or other math operators may produce this

```elixir
assert 2 * 2.0 === ___
```

## Two ways to round, are they exactly the same?

```elixir
assert Float.round(1.2) === round(1.2) == ___
```

## Release the decimals into the void

```elixir
assert trunc(5.6) === ___
```

## Are you odd?

```elixir
assert Integer.is_odd(3) == ___
```

## Actually you might be even

```elixir
assert Integer.is_even(4) == ___
```

## Let's grab the individual digits in a list

```elixir
individual_digits = Integer.digits(58_127)
```

```elixir
assert individual_digits == ___
```

## Oh no! I need it back together

```elixir
number = Integer.undigits([1, 2, 3, 4])
```

```elixir
assert number == ___
```

## Actually I want my number as a string

```elixir
string_digit = Integer.to_string(1234)
```

```elixir
assert string_digit == ___
```

## The meaning of life in hexadecimal is 2A!

```elixir
assert Integer.parse("2A", 16) == {___, ""}
```

## The remaining unparsable part is also returned

```elixir
assert Integer.parse("5 years") == {5, ___}
```

## What if you parse a floating point value as an integer?

```elixir
assert Integer.parse("1.2") == {___, ___}
```

## Just want to parse to a float

```elixir
assert Float.parse("34.5") == {___, ""}
```

## Hmm, I want to parse this but it has some strings

```elixir
assert Float.parse("1.5 million dollars") == {___, " million dollars"}
```

## I don't want this decimal point, let's round up

```elixir
assert Float.ceil(34.25) === ___
```

## OK, I only want it to 1 decimal place

```elixir
assert Float.ceil(34.25, 1) === ___
```

## Rounding down is what I need

```elixir
assert Float.floor(99.99) === ___
```

## Rounding down to 2 decimal places

```elixir
assert Float.floor(12.345, 2) === ___
```

## Round the number up or down for me

```elixir
assert Float.round(5.5) === ___
```

```elixir
assert Float.round(5.4) === ___
```

```elixir
assert Float.round(8.94, 1) === ___
```

```elixir
assert Float.round(-5.5674, 3) === ___
```

## I want the first and last in the range

```elixir
first..last//_step = Range.new(1, 10)
```

```elixir
assert first == ___
```

```elixir
assert last == ___
```

## Does my number exist in the range?

```elixir
def range?(%Range{}), do: true
def range?(_), do: false

range = Range.new(1, 10)
```

```elixir
assert 4 in range == ___
```

```elixir
assert 10 in range == ___
```

```elixir
assert 0 in range == ___
```

## Is this a range?

```elixir
assert range?(1..10) == ___
```

```elixir
assert range?(0) == ___
```

